  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 136 PM CDT WED SEP 9 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging will slowly degrade over the next 24-\n48 hours, as a trough and cold front begin to move through the\nMidwest. This cold front is still expected to stall somewhere\nalong north Tennessee, where it will remain through the weekend.\nBecause of this stalled cold front, and a series of weak\ndisturbances along the broken trough, rain chances will begin to\nincrease by Thursday afternoon, steadily increasing over the\nweekend. While weekend rain coverage may not be completely\nwidespread, it should be enough for many locations across the\nregion to see at least some measurable rainfall through Sunday.\n\nUnfortunately, upper-level ridging will quickly begin to\nstrengthen over the SE by Monday, dropping rainfall chances back\ndown to 25% or less. This activity will more resemble the typical\nscattered, diurnally driven thunderstorms we're used to seeing\nunder the influence of a ridge. Afternoon highs will quickly\nclimb under this pattern as well, with highs returning to the low\nand mid-90s for much of the region.\n\n/44/",
